WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.280
Just a few weeks ago, the federal government proposed a plan for a nearly $800 million

00:05.280 --> 00:07.880
infrastructure project in the state of Illinois.

00:07.880 --> 00:12.680
It's not about maintaining bridges or repaving highways, but it is about building barriers

00:12.680 --> 00:15.360
to defend Lake Michigan from a fish.

00:15.360 --> 00:19.840
It's the Asian carp, a fish that has already wreaked environmental havoc up and down the

00:19.840 --> 00:21.720
Mississippi River watershed.

00:21.720 --> 00:27.840
NewsHour Weekend's Megan Thompson has our report.

00:27.840 --> 00:32.680
Carly Gilpin Jr. fires up his boat and heads out to fish a stretch of the Illinois River,

00:32.680 --> 00:35.360
about 100 miles west of Chicago.

00:35.360 --> 00:39.600
He's spent most of his adult life working on the lakes and rivers of the upper Midwest.

00:39.600 --> 00:41.600
I'm a third generation commercial fisherman.

00:41.600 --> 00:45.080
I've been doing it for about 22, 23 years.

00:45.080 --> 00:48.240
Like a typical fisherman, Gilpin gets an early start.

00:48.240 --> 00:50.840
He drives while an assistant sets out the nets.

00:50.840 --> 00:52.840
You know what you got left?

00:52.840 --> 00:54.840
Not very much.

00:54.840 --> 00:55.840
Okay.

00:55.840 --> 00:58.480
Now let's see if we can scare him.

00:58.480 --> 00:59.720
What comes next?

00:59.720 --> 01:00.720
Not so typical.

01:00.720 --> 01:06.080
Go ahead, Doug.

01:06.080 --> 01:10.240
This is how you catch Asian carp.

01:10.240 --> 01:14.840
The loud sounds startle the fish and push them into the nets.

01:14.840 --> 01:16.640
The other unusual thing?

01:16.640 --> 01:19.400
These fish jump.

01:19.400 --> 01:24.640
The flopping, flailing fish are an invasive species wreaking havoc on lakes and rivers

01:24.640 --> 01:27.200
across the south and midwest.

01:27.200 --> 01:31.000
Gilpin and the other fishermen out here today are employed by the state of Illinois.

01:31.000 --> 01:32.000
Their mission?

01:32.000 --> 01:34.480
Pull as many carp out of the river as they can.

01:34.480 --> 01:36.640
Kevin Irons runs the program.

01:36.640 --> 01:41.280
So whenever any of these carps are out of balance, there's too many of them, they're

01:41.280 --> 01:43.760
going to have catastrophic ecological effects.

01:43.760 --> 01:45.560
That's our silver carp.

01:45.560 --> 01:50.440
There are four types of Asian carp, but bighead and silver cause the most trouble.

01:50.440 --> 01:52.880
They reproduce faster than other fish.

01:52.880 --> 01:56.880
They grow very quickly and can get to over 100 pounds.

01:56.880 --> 02:00.440
And they gobble up plankton and algae, pushing out the native fish.

02:00.440 --> 02:04.720
The carp were first brought to the southern U.S. from Asia in the 1960s to help clean

02:04.720 --> 02:09.440
ponds and fish farms, but some escaped to nearby rivers and lakes.

02:09.440 --> 02:15.840
So places that were once prized fisheries for fish like smallmouth bass or walleye become

02:15.840 --> 02:17.840
primarily Asian carp habitat.

02:17.840 --> 02:22.280
Joel Brammeyer heads the environmental nonprofit, The Alliance for the Great Lakes.

02:22.280 --> 02:27.080
Brammeyer explains that over the last three decades, the carp have moved up the Mississippi

02:27.080 --> 02:29.040
and through its tributaries.

02:29.040 --> 02:34.560
Today the leading edge of the population hovers about 50 miles from Lake Michigan.

02:34.560 --> 02:35.720
The fear now?

02:35.720 --> 02:41.800
The carp could get into Lake Michigan and then into the other four great lakes.

02:41.800 --> 02:47.520
Their most likely path is a series of canals dug in the Chicago area in the 1800s to connect

02:47.520 --> 02:50.720
Lake Michigan to the Mississippi River system.

02:50.720 --> 02:55.360
The canals provided new shipping lanes, carried away sewage and were hailed as a great feat

02:55.360 --> 02:56.360
of engineering.

02:56.360 --> 02:59.880
But it also created a whole set of new problems.

02:59.880 --> 03:04.840
Brammeyer says Asian carp in the Great Lakes could spell disaster for the region's environment

03:04.840 --> 03:09.280
and economy, which is highly dependent on tourism and sport fishing.

03:09.280 --> 03:13.160
We've got fish like lake trout and walleye and yellow perch that form the backbone of

03:13.160 --> 03:15.960
a $7 billion sport fishery.

03:15.960 --> 03:18.680
These fish are the kind that can get pushed out.

03:18.680 --> 03:22.160
And then there's that issue of all that jumping.

03:22.160 --> 03:25.920
And where they've really gotten established, they've basically shut down streams and lakes

03:25.920 --> 03:31.680
from recreation because for one, when you powerboat through Asian carp habitat, silver

03:31.680 --> 03:36.920
carp in particular jump out of the water and can actually hit people in the boats.

03:36.920 --> 03:41.520
Over the last two decades, officials across the upper Midwest have been sounding alarms

03:41.520 --> 03:45.960
and spending hundreds of millions of state and federal dollars to try to keep the carp

03:45.960 --> 03:46.960
out.

03:46.960 --> 03:52.280
Currently the biggest and most expensive defense lies in the Chicago Canal about 25 miles

03:52.280 --> 03:53.820
from Lake Michigan.

03:53.820 --> 03:57.040
We actually have three operating electrical barriers.

03:57.040 --> 04:01.520
Chuck Shea is a project manager for the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers, which operates a

04:01.520 --> 04:05.400
series of specially designed underwater electrical barriers.

04:05.400 --> 04:08.560
So the electricity is very rapidly turning on and off.

04:08.560 --> 04:13.960
Three sets of eight tons steel bars send electrical pulses into the water above.

04:13.960 --> 04:18.180
As the fish comes into the electrical field, it gets a stronger and stronger electrical

04:18.180 --> 04:21.200
shock and most fish will turn around.

04:21.200 --> 04:25.400
Shea says this is the largest barrier of its kind in the world.

04:25.400 --> 04:29.320
The Army Corps says the biggest danger is the risk of electrical shock to boaters who

04:29.320 --> 04:30.520
might pass through.

04:30.520 --> 04:33.560
They should watch any children or pets that are on board and everybody should stay away

04:33.560 --> 04:35.240
from the edges of the boat.

04:35.240 --> 04:42.960
The three fences and a fourth slated for 2021 cost $270 million to build.

04:42.960 --> 04:47.560
In addition to maintenance of the barriers, staffed around the clock cost around $16 million

04:47.560 --> 04:48.560
a year.

04:48.560 --> 04:53.360
Do you ever just look at this and think, oh my gosh, all of this for some fish?

04:53.360 --> 04:57.040
Well, it's more than just some fish though.

04:57.040 --> 05:02.320
There's an entire ecosystem in the Great Lakes that's of concern and that has great

05:02.320 --> 05:04.960
economic as well as environmental benefits.

05:04.960 --> 05:09.600
Shea says the barrier has been successful in protecting those benefits.

05:09.600 --> 05:14.160
This tagged fish downstream and have never seen any tagged fish get through, but shea

05:14.160 --> 05:17.000
admits the system's not foolproof.

05:17.000 --> 05:21.360
It would be very difficult for any fish to swim directly through the electrical fields

05:21.360 --> 05:26.360
when they're on and operating, but I don't know that we can say anything in life is absolutely

05:26.360 --> 05:29.080
100% effective.

05:29.080 --> 05:35.400
In fact, in 2017, an Asian carp was found beyond the barrier, about nine miles from entering

05:35.400 --> 05:36.800
Lake Michigan.

05:36.800 --> 05:41.360
In 2010, another carp was caught about six miles from Lake Michigan.

05:41.360 --> 05:46.640
Carp DNA has also been detected in the waterways and even in Lake Michigan itself.

05:46.640 --> 05:50.920
There's debate over whether the fish slipped through the barrier or made it into the waters

05:50.920 --> 05:52.760
some other way.

05:52.760 --> 05:56.720
The discoveries further stoked regional tensions over the issue.

05:56.720 --> 06:01.920
The state of Michigan, with its 3,300 miles of Great Lakes coastline, has long pressured

06:01.920 --> 06:05.120
Illinois to do more to stop the carp.

06:05.120 --> 06:11.120
In 2009, Michigan and four other states sued Illinois in a bid to shut down the Chicago

06:11.120 --> 06:14.120
canals to keep the carp out of Lake Michigan.

06:14.120 --> 06:19.200
The lawsuit went nowhere and other talk of taking the drastic step of closing the canals

06:19.200 --> 06:24.480
has met stiff opposition in Illinois because billions of dollars of freight travel through

06:24.480 --> 06:26.880
the area's waterways every year.

06:26.880 --> 06:32.200
In this region, we ship probably 25 to 35 million tons annually.

06:32.200 --> 06:36.240
Then direct tens of thousands of jobs are directly involved with this industry.

06:36.240 --> 06:40.680
Dell Wilkins is the president of Illinois Marine Towing, a major shipping company.

06:40.680 --> 06:46.000
He agrees Asian carp must be stopped, but not at the expense of the massive barges carrying

06:46.000 --> 06:50.520
everything from grain to fuel to cement up and down the rivers.

06:50.520 --> 06:55.640
We look at the Asian carp as a threat as well to the Lake Michigan region.

06:55.640 --> 07:01.000
Let's understand how we move to be able to mitigate that risk and find a way to have

07:01.000 --> 07:05.240
a win-win solution versus a win-lose solution.

07:05.240 --> 07:10.480
Evelyn Sanguennetti, Illinois' outgoing Republican Lieutenant Governor, agrees.

07:10.480 --> 07:13.800
She's helped oversee the state's battle against Asian carp.

07:13.800 --> 07:16.920
In our waterways, there is commerce.

07:16.920 --> 07:21.720
So that is a balance that oftentimes we're encountering.

07:21.720 --> 07:25.600
Since those discoveries of carp beyond the electric barrier, the state of Illinois has

07:25.600 --> 07:29.120
launched a number of projects to keep them out of the Great Lakes.

07:29.120 --> 07:32.280
Their favorite fishing spots can be ruined by aquatic invaders.

07:32.280 --> 07:34.320
Their public awareness campaigns.

07:34.320 --> 07:36.160
Remove, drain, dry.

07:36.160 --> 07:40.280
And regular monitoring of the waterways to keep tabs on where the fish are.

07:40.280 --> 07:44.600
It goes with that other campaign that we have, which goes something like this.

07:44.600 --> 07:47.560
If you can't beat them, then you eat them.

07:47.560 --> 07:51.720
That's right, the state's trying to get people to eat Asian carp, a fish that Americans have

07:51.720 --> 07:53.680
never embraced as food.

07:53.680 --> 07:58.920
A program at the University of Illinois serves up more than 10,000 pounds of carp every year

07:58.920 --> 08:00.240
in its dining halls.

08:00.240 --> 08:03.920
That's really good.

08:03.920 --> 08:08.640
But the state program that pays fishermen like Charlie Gilpin Jr. to catch as many fish

08:08.640 --> 08:11.520
as they can is making the biggest dent.

08:11.520 --> 08:16.040
Kevin Irons of the Department of Natural Resources helped launch it in 2010.

08:16.040 --> 08:18.680
All Asian carp all the time is our little mantra.

08:18.680 --> 08:23.560
The program's price tag of just over $1 million per year is covered by the U.S. Environmental

08:23.560 --> 08:25.240
Protection Agency.

08:25.240 --> 08:30.960
Nine teams of professional fishermen can each catch up to three tons of carp in a day,

08:30.960 --> 08:32.320
sometimes more.

08:32.320 --> 08:35.440
Biologists ride along to document the catch.

08:35.440 --> 08:40.080
Charlie Gilpin Jr. used to catch and sell buffalo fish in western Illinois where he's

08:40.080 --> 08:44.640
from, but then the Asian carp arrived about 15 years ago.

08:44.640 --> 08:49.840
In those areas, you can't target the native fish that you used to because of the abundance

08:49.840 --> 08:51.040
of the Asian carp.

08:51.040 --> 08:55.200
So you kind of had to join in and just catch the Asian carp.

08:55.200 --> 09:00.160
Gilpin says before this program, he was struggling to make a living fishing, let alone on Asian

09:00.160 --> 09:03.120
carp, which hasn't caught on widely as food.

09:03.120 --> 09:05.640
So this program has helped him out a lot.

09:05.640 --> 09:08.640
And his father, who's out here fishing too.

09:08.640 --> 09:12.200
At the end of each day, the fishermen tow their catch to a nearby town.

09:12.200 --> 09:16.560
The carp are transferred to huge bins and stored in a refrigerated truck.

09:16.560 --> 09:19.120
Anyone can come get them free of charge.

09:19.120 --> 09:23.860
They just can't be eaten by people since they win a few hours without being refrigerated.

09:23.860 --> 09:27.340
So they're made into things like fish fertilizer and bait.

09:27.340 --> 09:31.740
The day we were there, a pet food maker showed up to gladly take the free ingredients.

09:31.740 --> 09:35.620
If we load up, I got cooler device, we're going to ice it down, tarp it, and I'm going

09:35.620 --> 09:37.580
to take it home and get it processed.

09:37.580 --> 09:44.260
Since this program started in 2010, the state says it has caught 7.5 million pounds of carp,

09:44.260 --> 09:48.340
decreasing the leading edge population by 93%.

09:48.340 --> 09:53.260
Do you hope someday that you can just stop fishing for Asian carp in this part of the river?

09:53.260 --> 09:54.380
It'd be great, right?

09:54.380 --> 09:58.420
I'm concerned with people thinking eradication is the ultimate goal.

09:58.420 --> 10:00.420
It's really, really hard to do.

10:00.420 --> 10:04.900
And that's why there's a whole new line of carp defenses being planned at the Brandon

10:04.900 --> 10:09.660
Road Lock and Dam, about 10 miles before the existing electric barriers.

10:09.660 --> 10:14.940
The Army Corps is proposing to install more electric barriers, underwater sound machines

10:14.940 --> 10:19.700
to scare the fish, and something called an air bubble curtain to flush out any carp caught

10:19.700 --> 10:22.180
between barges traveling through.

10:22.180 --> 10:26.260
The state of Illinois has agreed to sponsor the project reluctantly, worried about the

10:26.260 --> 10:27.900
costs.

10:27.900 --> 10:33.120
It's very easy to automatically come to the conclusion that the sky is falling.

10:33.120 --> 10:38.460
And so this is why it's so important that we not have a knee-jerk reaction to this.

10:38.460 --> 10:41.500
You can stop Asian carp from devastating the Great Lakes.

10:41.500 --> 10:46.260
Meanwhile, the state of Michigan is gung-ho, even launching a campaign to pressure Illinois

10:46.260 --> 10:48.420
to take a stronger stand.

10:48.420 --> 10:52.260
All the states need to come together and not make money the issue.

10:52.260 --> 10:54.220
The issue is these carp.

10:54.220 --> 10:55.940
But the money is an issue.

10:55.940 --> 10:59.380
A new price tag for the project was announced in November.

10:59.380 --> 11:04.780
Nearly $778 million, three times the original estimate.

11:04.780 --> 11:08.340
Congress must now approve the plan and provide some funding.

11:08.340 --> 11:12.460
Previous talks between Illinois and Michigan about sharing the remaining costs are now

11:12.460 --> 11:17.260
up in the air, given the new price tag and the fact that new governors are taking over

11:17.260 --> 11:20.820
both states this month.

11:20.820 --> 11:25.100
While the politicians sort it all out, the fishermen in Illinois continue their work

11:25.100 --> 11:29.100
hauling ton after ton of big head and silver carp.

11:29.100 --> 11:33.860
Charlie Gilpin Jr. says he's proud to be a part of the mission and even sees a silver

11:33.860 --> 11:37.820
lining for what he calls his dying profession.

11:37.820 --> 11:40.940
With the abundance of the fish, it's kind of giving us another outlet.

11:40.940 --> 11:43.540
It's kind of a blessing and a curse.

11:43.540 --> 11:44.540
It may be our savior.

11:44.540 --> 11:45.540
Thank you.
